nodecode-telegram-stickers is a rewrite of churnast's churnast/hermes-telegram-stickers:

  https://github.com/churnast/hermes-telegram-stickers
  commit 210ce860a06ed24b87f34d9c418f544d2afde4f0
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 churnast (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the model-facing half of the plugin - telegram_sticker_find, telegram_sticker_send and
telegram_sticker_settings with their argument schemas and answer shapes, the /stickers command's
listing, reload and per-chat off switch, the getStickerSet and sendSticker calls and the ids
('pack_name:12') a find answers with, the reaction table that lets words ('facepalm', 'eye roll',
'thumbs up') and feelings find a sticker in packs without that exact emoji, the nearest-feeling
fallback, the four Telegram-made packs used while no pack is set, the per-chat cooldown, the
'mute a chat' switch, and the rule that a tool error carries no bot token. The Python (asyncio,
python-telegram-bot) is Common Lisp here: nlk:http for the Bot API, one section declaration for
the settings, four-tool shapes as nlk:define-contract declarations, and in-memory catalogs and
pacing in place of the plugin's JSON files.

Left out: everything that needs the Hermes gateway. The plugin knows which chat, forum topic and
message a turn belongs to, so its send needs no chat id; a cell has no turn-level Telegram
context, and takes chat_id (or the section's default_chat_id) and reply_to_message_id instead.
The plugin's sticker-observer handler, which learns packs from stickers sent to the bot in a direct
chat, and its pre_llm_call hint and in-chat setup walk have no equivalent here - a cell registers
no gateway handlers and sees no turns - so the packs are the ones the section names. Also left out:
the vision pass (/stickers describe) that described each sticker picture with the gateway's vision
model and stored the descriptions, so picking by meaning here is the emoji tags, the reaction table
and pack names rather than a description of the picture; the per-chat pace set in words, the ban
list, the forget-a-pack and forget-all actions; and the on-disk catalog, descriptions and pacing
state, which this cell keeps in memory for the life of the process.
